### In-Depth Study: Core Methods of Thread Class & Context Switching

#### 1. Detailed Method Mechanisms & Exceptions:
- **`sleep(long millis)`:** Puts the executing thread into the `TIMED_WAITING` state. Throws `InterruptedException` if another thread interrupts it while sleeping. Crucially, **it does not release locks**.
- **`join()`:** Allows one thread to wait for the completion of another. Used extensively in master-worker patterns where the main thread must aggregate results after worker threads complete.
- **`isAlive()`:** Returns a boolean indicating whether the thread is alive (it has started and has not yet died).
- **`interrupt()`:** Sets the thread's interrupt status flag. If the thread is blocked in `sleep()` or `wait()`, it wakes up and throws `InterruptedException`.

#### 2. Complete Context-Switching & Thread Methods Java Program:
```java
public class ThreadMethodsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread worker1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
                System.out.println("Worker 1 - Step " + i);
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(200); // Context switch via sleep
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    System.err.println("Worker 1 interrupted");
                }
            }
        });

        Thread worker2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
                System.out.println("Worker 2 - Step " + i);
                Thread.yield(); // Cooperative yielding
            }
        });

        System.out.println("Worker 1 isAlive before start: " + worker1.isAlive());

        worker1.start();
        worker2.start();

        System.out.println("Worker 1 isAlive after start: " + worker1.isAlive());

        try {
            // Main thread waits for both workers to complete
            worker1.join();
            worker2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.err.println("Main interrupted while joining: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("Worker 1 isAlive after join: " + worker1.isAlive());
        System.out.println("All threads finished. Main thread exiting.");
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Methods like join() and sleep() provide deterministic coordination of asynchronous threads.}$$
